    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "In rectangle $ABCD$, if one vertex of $\\triangle A_1B_1C_1$ falls on $A$ and another vertex on $C$,  then it is impossible to draw an equilateral triangle as no point of the triangle can lie inside given rectangle.\n\nSo one of the points of $\\triangle A_2B_2C_2$ has to lie on the vertices of the rectangle. There are $4$ possibilites: one vertex on $B$, or $AC$ or $AD$ or both. Let this be point $A$. Then the area of $\\triangle A_2B_2C_2$ is $5 \\cdot \\frac{ \\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 5 = \\frac{25 \\sqrt{3}}{4}$.\n\nElevation Diagram:\nhttps://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/File:4257-BRA1998-Figure.jpg?2928\n\nOnce there is a point of an equilateral triangle at one of the vertices of rectangle, The maximum area of the equilateral triangle comprised within the rectangle occurs when the third vertex of the equilateral triangle is the other vertex of the rectangle creating a line segment as its common sides to rectangle.\n\nAnswer box must be $\\boxed{\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}}$. \n\nSource: http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/blog.php?u=527273&b=672556&utm_source=Solution%20of%20Mathematics%20Competition%20Pratice%202020%20Grades%208-10%20(2nd%20edition)%20Exam%20B&p=672512&mode=view\n\n[asy] import graph; size(6cm); real labelscalefactor = 0.5; node(scalefactor); defaultpen(fontsize(8)); pen dps = linewidth(0.7) + fontsize(8); real xmin = -3.397601363838239, xmax = 26.426308426030491, ymin = -4.7395755610622885, ymax = 20.865964741350877;  draw((0,0)--(14,0)--(6,4)--(6,0), linewidth(2)); draw((0,0)--(2,2)--(4,2)--(6,2)--(6,4)--(14,0), linewidth(2)); label(\"$B$\", (6,0), S); label(\"$A$\", (0,0), SW); label(\"$C$\", (14,0), SE); label(\"$D$\", (6,4), W);  label(\"$P$\", (14/6, 0), N); dot((14/6, 0),linewidth(3));  MP(\"1\", (2/6,2/6)); MP(\"1\", (4/6,2/6)); MP(\"1\", (32/6,7/6)); MP(\"10\", (5/6,3/6)); [/asy]\nGiven a rectangle \\(ABCD\\) with side lengths \\(10\\) and \\(11\\) units, we need to find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle comprised within this rectangle.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n1. **Analyze the Rectangle Configuration:**\n   - The vertices of the rectangle are \\(A = (0, 0)\\), \\(B = (14, 0)\\), \\(C = (6, 4)\\), and \\(D = (6, 0)\\).\n\n2. **Identify the Maximum Area Configuration:**\n   - The maximum area equilateral triangle within the rectangle can be constructed by placing one vertex at a corner of the rectangle and another vertex on an adjacent side.\n\n3. **Place Vertex \\(P\\) on \\(AB:**\n   - Let \\(P = \\left(\\frac{14}{3}, 0\\right)\\).\n\n4. **Determine the Height of the Triangle:**\n   - In an equilateral triangle, all sides are equal. We need to determine the height \\(h\\).\n\n5. **Calculate the Height \\(h\\) using the Pythagorean Theorem:**\n   \\[\n   h^2 + \\left(\\frac{6}{3}\\right)^2 = 10^2\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(h\\):\n   \\[\n   h^2 + 2^2 = 10^2 \\implies h^2 + 4 = 100 \\implies h^2 = 96 \\implies h = \\sqrt{96} = 4\\sqrt{6}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Verify the Area Calculation:**\n   - The area of an equilateral triangle with height \\(h\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} h^2\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(h = 4\\sqrt{6}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} (4\\sqrt{6})^2\n   \\]\n   Simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   \\left(4\\sqrt{6}\\right)^2 = 16 \\cdot 6 = 96\n   \\]\n   Hence,\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot 96 = 24\\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible area of the equilateral triangle is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\\).",
